{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n51 \n \n \nexamined for use in calculating the soot volume fraction.  Intensified camera images have \nyet to be analyzed. \n \nThe raw non-imaging data acquired from round 2 of Flame Design testing (e.g., gas flow \nrates, burner surface temperature, photomultiplier tube measurements of flame \nemissions, radiometer measurements of flame thermal emission, far-field gas \ntemperatures, and pressures) has been converted to engineering units.  All the raw (hobj) \nimages obtained from the ACME camera have been successfully converted to tiff files for \nfurther analysis.  Thin filament pyrometry is being used to obtain fiber temperature \nmeasurements in ACME camera images that have SiC fibers inserted in the flame.  Burn \ntime and extinction diameter have been acquired for radiatively extinguishing flames \nusing the photomultiplier tubes and analog camera, respectively.  Intensified camera \nimages have yet to be analyzed. \n \nA one-dimensional computational model is being implemented to assist in the \ninvestigation of spherical diffusion flames in microgravity.  Also, collaborations are \nunderway with scientists at the Russian Academy of Sciences for further simulations.  The \nRussian team is focusing on the porous spherical burner and more realistic boundary \nconditions, while the US team is focusing on a general understanding of the flame \ndynamics. \n \nK. Preliminary Research Findings \nFlame Design results have shown that spherical diffusion flames in microgravity have \nunique characteristics that are valuable for fundamental studies of diffusion flames. \nFlame size increases with reactant flow rate and decreases with ambient oxygen \nconcentration.  Radiative heat loss scales with flame size such that very small flames - just \nabove their kinetic extinction limits - have low radiative loss, and asymptotically approach \nsteady-state behavior.  Larger flames increase in size over time until the rate of radiative \nheat loss becomes sufficiently large, and the flame extinguishes via radiative extinction.  \nBefore total extinction occurs in these flames, they experience an unstable, oscillatory \nmode, in which they partially extinguish and reform.  The oscillations grow in magnitude \nuntil total extinction.  For constant ambient oxygen concentration, larger flames (with \nhigher fuel flow rates) radiatively extinguish faster than smaller flames (with lower flow", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 51}}